 <div class="doc-subhead">Region</div>
 <p class="doc-para">A geographic area (e.g., "US East," "UK South") containing multiple isolated data-center facilities. All four providers use the word "Region" the same way. <strong>Mapping Quality:</strong> Strong Match. <strong>Memory trick:</strong> Region = the city/metro area. <strong>Important difference:</strong> the number and geographic footprint of regions differs a lot between providers -- always check current availability for your workload before assuming a region exists.</p>
 <div class="doc-subhead">Availability Zone / Availability Domain</div>
-<p class="doc-para">An isolated failure domain inside a region: its own power, cooling, and networking, so that one physical outage does not take down everything in the region. AWS and Azure call it an Availability Zone (AZ); GCP calls it a Zone; OCI calls it an Availability Domain (AD). <strong>Mapping Quality:</strong> Strong Match. <strong>Important difference:</strong> OCI historically shipped with far fewer Availability Domains per region than AWS/Azure/GCP have AZs -- some OCI regions have only 1 AD. OCI compensates with Fault Domains inside each AD. Do not assume "3 AZs" the way you might for AWS.</p>
+<p class="doc-para">An isolated failure domain inside a region: its own power, cooling, and networking, so that one physical outage does not take down everything in the region. AWS and Azure call it an Availability Zone (AZ). GCP calls it a Zone. OCI calls it an Availability Domain (AD). <strong>Mapping Quality:</strong> Strong Match. <strong>Important difference:</strong> OCI historically shipped with far fewer Availability Domains per region than AWS/Azure/GCP have AZs -- some OCI regions have only 1 AD. OCI compensates with Fault Domains inside each AD. Do not assume "3 AZs" the way you might for AWS.</p>
 <div class="doc-subhead">Fault Domain</div>
 <p class="doc-para">A smaller failure-isolation boundary inside an Availability Zone/Domain -- separate racks, power, and hardware, so redundant copies of something do not fail together even within the same zone. <strong>Mapping Quality:</strong> Concept Only. OCI is the provider that leans on this concept most heavily: every OCI Availability Domain is explicitly divided into 3 Fault Domains, and OCI's own high-availability guidance assumes you spread instances across them. <strong>Memory trick:</strong> Region, then Zone/AD, then Fault Domain -- each level is a smaller box inside the last one.</p>
