 <div class="doc-subhead" id="encryption">7. Encryption and Key Management</div>
 <div class="doc-subhead">What Encryption Actually Protects (and What It Does Not)</div>
 <p class="doc-para">Encryption protects data confidentiality if the underlying storage or network is somehow accessed directly, bypassing your application (a stolen disk, a misconfigured public bucket, a network sniffer). <strong>It does not replace access control.</strong> If a user has valid IAM permission to read a file, encryption at rest is transparent to them -- they see the plaintext, because the service decrypts it for them. Encryption and IAM solve different problems and you need both.</p>
-<div class="doc-table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Simple meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Encryption at rest</td><td>Protects stored data -- on disk, in a database, in an object store, in a backup.</td></tr><tr><td>Encryption in transit</td><td>Protects data while it moves across a network -- usually TLS for application traffic, IPsec for VPNs.</td></tr><tr><td>Provider-managed keys</td><td>The cloud provider generates, stores, and rotates the encryption key for you. Simplest, but you do not directly control it.</td></tr><tr><td>Customer-managed keys (CMK)</td><td>You create and control the key (rotation, disabling, deletion, access policy) inside the provider's KMS; the provider's service still performs the actual encrypt/decrypt using that key.</td></tr></table></div>
+<div class="doc-table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Simple meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Encryption at rest</td><td>Protects stored data -- on disk, in a database, in an object store, in a backup.</td></tr><tr><td>Encryption in transit</td><td>Protects data while it moves across a network -- usually TLS for application traffic, IPsec for VPNs.</td></tr><tr><td>Provider-managed keys</td><td>The cloud provider generates, stores, and rotates the encryption key for you. Simplest, but you do not directly control it.</td></tr><tr><td>Customer-managed keys (CMK)</td><td>You create and control the key (rotation, disabling, deletion, access policy) inside the provider's KMS. The provider's service still performs the actual encrypt/decrypt using that key.</td></tr></table></div>
 <p class="doc-para"><strong>Who controls the key, and why it matters:</strong> with a provider-managed key, the provider can always decrypt your data on your behalf -- you are trusting their operational and legal controls. With a customer-managed key, you decide who and what can use the key, and can revoke that access (or delete the key) to make data cryptographically unreadable even to the provider's own service -- but now key availability is your responsibility too. Do not say customer-managed keys are simply "more secure" without qualification.</p>
