 <ul class="doc-list"><li><strong>High Availability (HA):</strong> surviving a local failure (a host, a rack, a zone) automatically, usually within seconds, without anyone declaring an incident. Achieved by spreading redundant components across Availability Zones/Domains.</li><li><strong>Backup:</strong> a separate, recoverable copy of data, protecting against corruption, accidental deletion, or ransomware. A backup does not by itself restore a running service -- someone has to actually restore it.</li><li><strong>Disaster Recovery (DR):</strong> the plan and tooling to bring an entire application/service back online, usually in a different region or site, after a large-scale failure. DR is orchestration on top of replicated infrastructure and/or backups -- a business-continuity capability, not a single button.</li></ul>
 <p class="doc-para"><strong>Backup is not Disaster Recovery. High Availability is not Disaster Recovery.</strong> You can have excellent backups and still have no real DR plan if nobody has practiced restoring the whole application stack, in order, within a time limit.</p>
