 <div class="doc-subhead">Metrics vs. Logs vs. Traces vs. Audit Logs</div>
 <p class="doc-para">Metrics are numbers over time (CPU%, request count). Logs are detailed text records of events. Traces follow one request's path across multiple services to find where time was spent or where it failed. Audit logs record who took what administrative/API action, for accountability and compliance, a fundamentally different purpose than the other three, which are about system behavior, not human accountability.</p>
 <div class="doc-subhead">Secret vs. Encryption Key</div>
-<p class="doc-para">A secret is a piece of sensitive data your application needs (a database password, an API token). An encryption key is the cryptographic material used to encrypt/decrypt other data. A secrets manager <em>stores</em> secrets (and can itself use an encryption key to protect them at rest); a KMS <em>manages</em> encryption keys. Some providers (Azure Key Vault, for example) put both under one product umbrella, which makes this distinction easy to blur. Keep the two separate anyway.</p>
+<p class="doc-para">A secret is a piece of sensitive data your application needs (a database password, an API token). An encryption key is the cryptographic material used to encrypt/decrypt other data. A secrets manager <em>stores</em> secrets (and can itself use an encryption key to protect them at rest), while a KMS <em>manages</em> encryption keys. Some providers (Azure Key Vault, for example) put both under one product umbrella, which makes this distinction easy to blur. Keep the two separate anyway.</p>
 <div class="doc-subhead">Kubernetes vs. Serverless</div>
 <p class="doc-para">Kubernetes gives you a persistent, configurable, always-on orchestration platform where you (or a managed control plane) decide scaling, scheduling, and networking in detail. Serverless (functions or serverless containers) hands scaling and infrastructure decisions entirely to the platform, scales to zero when idle, and typically bills per invocation/duration rather than per running node. Neither is strictly "better", they trade control for operational simplicity in opposite directions.</p>
 <div class="doc-subhead">Availability Zone vs. Region</div>
-<p class="doc-para">A Region is a broad geographic area; an Availability Zone (or Availability Domain on OCI) is an isolated failure domain <em>inside</em> that region. Multi-zone protects against a local outage; multi-region protects against losing an entire geographic area (and is usually part of a DR strategy, not just an HA one).</p>
+<p class="doc-para">A Region is a broad geographic area. An Availability Zone (or Availability Domain on OCI) is an isolated failure domain <em>inside</em> that region. Multi-zone protects against a local outage. Multi-region protects against losing an entire geographic area (and is usually part of a DR strategy, not just an HA one).</p>
 <div class="doc-subhead">Snapshot vs. Backup</div>
 <p class="doc-para">A snapshot is typically a point-in-time, often incremental copy tied closely to the underlying storage/disk system, and can be very fast to create and restore from. A "backup" (in the AWS Backup / Azure Backup / centralized-policy sense) usually adds policy-driven scheduling, retention rules, and cross-service/cross-region consistency management on top of the same underlying snapshot mechanism. In casual conversation people use the words interchangeably. In an interview, it's worth pointing out that "a snapshot exists" and "we have a governed backup policy" are not the same claim.</p>
