         <div class="doc-section" id="corrections">
           <div class="doc-section-title">Corrections to Common Comparison Mistakes</div>
 <p class="doc-para">A lot of multi-cloud cheat sheets circulate online and in study groups, and several of them repeat the same mistakes. Below are the ones worth calling out directly, with the reasoning, so you don't repeat them either.</p>
-<ol class="doc-list"><li><strong>AWS Snow Family status.</strong> Older comparison charts list "AWS Snowball/Snow Family, Azure Data Box, and OCI Data Transfer Appliance" as a stable three-way match with no caveat. Official AWS documentation confirms Snowball Edge devices have been <strong>discontinued for new customer orders</strong>, with AWS directing new customers to AWS Data Transfer Terminal (offline) and AWS DataSync (online) instead. Corrected in the Migration section.</li><li><strong>Microsoft's AI platform rebrand.</strong> Charts still floating around reference only "Azure AI Foundry" or "Azure AI Studio." Neither is current: Microsoft rebranded this platform to <strong>Microsoft Foundry</strong> in 2026. Corrected in the Data, Analytics, and AI section, with the naming history kept so readers coming from older material aren't confused.</li><li><strong>Azure Cache for Redis retirement.</strong> Older material lists "Azure Cache for Redis" as Azure's current Redis offering with no qualification. Official Microsoft documentation confirms Azure Cache for Redis is being retired on a published timeline (2027-2028 depending on tier) in favor of <strong>Azure Managed Redis</strong>, which this guide treats as the current answer.</li><li><strong>Microsoft Fabric vs. Azure Synapse Analytics.</strong> These two are often either presented as unrelated products or Synapse is written off as already gone. Neither is accurate: official Microsoft messaging confirms Synapse Analytics remains supported, but Microsoft is actively guiding customers to migrate workloads to Fabric, which is now the primary strategic data platform.</li><li><strong>A common IAM table is materially misleading.</strong> It's common to see "OCI IAM / AWS IAM / Microsoft Entra ID / Cloud IAM" listed side by side as if they were four peer products solving the same problem. They are not. Entra ID and Cloud Identity are directory/identity-provider products; AWS IAM, Azure RBAC (evaluated against Entra ID identities), and Cloud IAM are authorization engines; OCI's Identity Domains uniquely bundle both roles into one product. This guide corrects that into an explicit two-layer model, with OCI's hybrid architecture called out (Identity and Access Management section).</li><li><strong>Audit-logging comparisons are often oversimplified.</strong> It's common to see "Azure Activity Log" listed as a straight peer to AWS CloudTrail with no distinction. Official Microsoft documentation shows Azure splits this responsibility between the Activity Log (resource-management operations) and Entra ID's own sign-in/audit logs (identity events). CloudTrail, Cloud Audit Logs, and OCI Audit are each closer to a single, unified log. Corrected in the Monitoring, Logging, and Audit section.</li><li><strong>OCI's resource hierarchy is not a flat equivalence.</strong> A common memory-trick slide states "OCI Compartment = AWS Account/OU = Azure Subscription = GCP Project" as if they're interchangeable. They're not: a Compartment is a logical subdivision <em>inside</em> one OCI Tenancy, while an AWS Account, Azure Subscription, and GCP Project are each themselves the base billing/quota unit (closer in level to the Tenancy itself). This is corrected throughout, most fully in the Cloud Fundamentals section, because it's one of the most common real-world sources of confusion for people moving between OCI and the other three providers.</li><li><strong>AWS PrivateLink does not have a single OCI equivalent.</strong> It's common to see a rough 1:1 drawn between AWS PrivateLink/Azure Private Link/GCP Private Service Connect and an OCI "Service Gateway / private endpoint" catch-all. This guide is explicit that OCI splits this job across Service Gateway (for OCI's own public services) and separate per-service private endpoint features, with no single unifying OCI product. Labeled Closest Match rather than Strong Match, with the distinction spelled out.</li><li><strong>Google Cloud Functions naming.</strong> Material that still calls this product only "Cloud Functions" is out of date. <strong>Cloud Run functions is the current official name</strong> (Google renamed Cloud Functions and re-platformed it onto Cloud Run infrastructure). Same product line, older name.</li><li><strong>API Gateway vs. Apigee on GCP.</strong> These two get listed as interchangeable alternatives for the same job more often than not. Official Google documentation is clear they are two distinct products for two different scopes: lightweight serverless API fronting versus full enterprise API lifecycle management. Corrected in the DevOps and Application Integration section.</li><li><strong>OCI has no direct Batch or wide-column NoSQL equivalent.</strong> It's tempting to stretch and name some loosely-related OCI feature as the answer for AWS Batch/Azure Batch/Google Cloud Batch, or for AWS Keyspaces/GCP Bigtable. Per this guide's own rule (if there's no reasonable equivalent, say so), both are labeled <strong>No direct equivalent</strong> for OCI instead.</li></ol>
+<ol class="doc-list"><li><strong>AWS Snow Family status.</strong> Older comparison charts list "AWS Snowball/Snow Family, Azure Data Box, and OCI Data Transfer Appliance" as a stable three-way match with no caveat. Official AWS documentation confirms Snowball Edge devices have been <strong>discontinued for new customer orders</strong>, with AWS directing new customers to AWS Data Transfer Terminal (offline) and AWS DataSync (online) instead. Corrected in the Migration section.</li><li><strong>Microsoft's AI platform rebrand.</strong> Charts still floating around reference only "Azure AI Foundry" or "Azure AI Studio." Neither is current: Microsoft rebranded this platform to <strong>Microsoft Foundry</strong> in 2026. Corrected in the Data, Analytics, and AI section, with the naming history kept so readers coming from older material aren't confused.</li><li><strong>Azure Cache for Redis retirement.</strong> Older material lists "Azure Cache for Redis" as Azure's current Redis offering with no qualification. Official Microsoft documentation confirms Azure Cache for Redis is being retired on a published timeline (2027-2028 depending on tier) in favor of <strong>Azure Managed Redis</strong>, which this guide treats as the current answer.</li><li><strong>Microsoft Fabric vs. Azure Synapse Analytics.</strong> These two are often either presented as unrelated products or Synapse is written off as already gone. Neither is accurate: official Microsoft messaging confirms Synapse Analytics remains supported, but Microsoft is actively guiding customers to migrate workloads to Fabric, which is now the primary strategic data platform.</li><li><strong>A common IAM table is materially misleading.</strong> It's common to see "OCI IAM / AWS IAM / Microsoft Entra ID / Cloud IAM" listed side by side as if they were four peer products solving the same problem. They are not. Entra ID and Cloud Identity are directory/identity-provider products. AWS IAM, Azure RBAC (evaluated against Entra